{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791039243,"nonce":"spJPEk1qduDyyEyfQiYctj5LY4Y=","title":"Every theme's shadow_inner token draws an OUTER shadow","message":"`Shadow` carries offset, blur, spread and colour, with no inset flag, and\nthe element paint walk draws every entry in `RenderProps.shadow` through\n`draw_shadow`. So an inner shadow is impossible to express on an element.\n\nThat is not hypothetical. All three universal themes define a\n`shadow_inner` token — hybrid, restrained and expressive, light and dark\n— and each one currently paints as a small drop shadow beneath the\nelement instead of an inset one. Anything styled with it is wrong today.\n\nInner shadows do exist, as the canvas-level `draw_inner_shadow`, declared\non the same `DrawContext` trait the paint walk already uses. So the\ncapability is reachable; nothing routes to it.\n\n\nReported by the ashui session, which hit it porting the theme's\n`shadowInner`. Its request is `Shadow { inset: bool }` plus a paint walk\nthat honours it.\n\nThere are two `Shadow` types and both need the field:\n`blinc_core::Shadow` (the render type) and `blinc_theme::Shadow` (the\ntoken type), with the `From` conversion carrying it. `blinc_theme`'s\n`new` is a 5-arg `const fn`, so an inset constructor has to be separate\nto keep it source-compatible.\n\nThe CSS parser has no `inset` keyword in `box-shadow` at all, so that is\nadditive too.\n\n\nCSS paints outer shadows under the background, then the background, then\ninner shadows, then the border. The paint walk merges fill and border\ninto ONE SDF primitive when the border is uniform, specifically to avoid\nAA fringe where they overlap at corners. With one primitive there is no\n\"between\" to insert inner shadows into.\n\nSo honouring CSS order for a bordered element means not merging when an\ninset shadow is present, trading that AA fringe back. Drawing inner\nshadows after the merged primitive instead is correct for the common\ncase — an inset well with no border — and slightly wrong for a bordered\none, where the shadow would sit over the border's inner edge.\n\nWorth deciding deliberately rather than discovering later.","files":null}]}